# Responsive scripts dashboard

Discovers adjacent `.man` manuals under `bin`, `dev`, and `sys`, then renders
their Markdown, typed parameters, warnings, progress, files, and command output
in one keyboard- and mouse-friendly interface.

> **Apply safety:** Destructive commands always preview first and require
> `CLEAN`. Cancel and Quit are disabled during apply because interrupting
> filesystem or Git maintenance can corrupt data.

External process termination is still outside the dashboard's control.
Elevated Linux/macOS repairs fail fast unless sudo/doas policy permits
non-interactive use; a normal terminal credential cache usually does not carry
into the isolated child process. Run the displayed command directly when a
password is required. Launch the Windows dashboard from an Administrator
terminal for privileged actions. Commands that need their own interactive
terminal are previewed in the dashboard, which then shows the direct apply
command.

Without `--root`, the dashboard uses a local checkout when one can be found;
otherwise it downloads a temporary copy of the repository and removes it when
the dashboard closes. Running requires `uv`; network access is needed only when
the pinned Textual dependency is not cached or a repository copy must be
downloaded.

The dashboard colors headings, safety callouts, tables, links, and code with
the selected terminal theme. The `.man` source remains plain Markdown, so it
also works through `curl`, Glow, GitHub, and terminals without color.

## Options

| Option | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `--root PATH` | use one local scripts checkout |
| `--inline` | run below the prompt on macOS or Linux |
| `--nerd-fonts` | use Nerd Font file and folder icons |
| `--self-test` | validate every manual contract and compact/wide layouts |

## Direct CLI

```sh
uv run dev/gui.py
uv run dev/gui.py --root .
uv run dev/gui.py --inline --nerd-fonts
uv run dev/gui.py --root . --self-test
```

## Exit status

`0` means the dashboard or self-test closed normally. `1` means repository or
manual discovery failed. Invalid command-line syntax exits `2`.
